That, like, basically, because you only have four different roads of tiers to jump across

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:43.100
and stuff like that, there's no, like, there's no puzzle element to this that Pac-Man presents

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.380
and, like, it's three to four different maze layouts.

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:51.460
Like, yes, the stages are a little different in terms of, like, the boundaries of the platforms themselves.

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:54.700
But I would have almost liked some dead ends that you could crash into

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:57.720
or, like, some different layers of tiers.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:14:01.480
Like, this one, you know, requires more precise jumps

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:04.480
or this one's just, like, you got to know about it first.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:06.600
You got to pass by it and then know, like, okay, cool.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:12.340
I got to jump up to first, third, and then I'll drop down into, like, this box to get into the second tier, you know?

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:16.740
Yeah. Just some kind of puzzle element to it for variety in the stages.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:20.660
If it was just the same stage over and over again, I almost would have probably accepted that.

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:21.960
Like, I would have been like, oh, that's it.

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:25.060
And then it's some nice scenery backdrop, and this is a high-score game.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:30.480
But instead, it offers just enough, like, offering of differences that I'm like,

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:32.740
well, why not just go even a little further then?

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:38.920
It's just the design philosophy that I was really hoping that we would get rid of for good

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:45.440
because we've been moving away from it, and that's just the random dumping of obstacles

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:49.740
with no pattern, no thought to.

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:51.160
No design. Yeah, no design.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:54.300
And just saying, like, avoid them.

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:55.420
Like, just stop.

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:56.840
Yeah. Right.

00:14:56.840 --> 00:15:02.880
And we've talked about the cat thing, but I think it's worth bringing up that the cat thing,

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:07.480
to me, kind of said a lot about just, like, the design philosophy of the game.

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:13.000
Because even if you didn't have the problem of it showing up in random places and stuff

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:17.360
like that, which definitely happened for me, I think what's weirder is that it doesn't

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:22.520
really communicate to you when you hit this cat that, like, something bad has happened.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:25.980
And it looks vulnerable until you lose a life.

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:30.500
Yeah, I think it's like punishment for hitting an innocent animal that teleports.

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:33.980
It almost looks like, you know, if you didn't know better, if you didn't look at the manual

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:37.340
and know that that was the cat or something, you could think that that's, like, some kind

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:41.640
of balloon thing that you've now, like, hit, and it's, like, going around the stage, and

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:46.180
it's like, oh, cool, it's gonna, like, anything it touches will, like, fill in, you know, or

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:50.920
something like that. But when you restart the stage again, you're like, huh, did I die?

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:52.600
You have to check your lives yourself.

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:55.700
There's nothing, basically, if you hit the cat three times in a row without ever knowing

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:57.740
that, you just get a game over screen all of a sudden.

00:15:57.820 --> 00:15:59.880
You're like, oh, I was killing a cat.

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:01.820
You had no idea, you know?

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:06.540
Like, I thought that was strange, because it just chooses what to communicate to you and

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:10.920
basically chooses nothing other than this, like, ridiculous miles system.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:13.180
Yeah, I don't really get the miles.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:15.680
At the top, which is just bizarre.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:21.540
I feel like it's kind of cool that they included, like, a, look at your car's mileage and stuff

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:23.440
like that, and you have this amount of oil.

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:30.420
But, like, I don't know, like, it just seems silly to gamify it in that way.

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:31.740
Like, just focus on score.

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:33.440
It's almost like you have to track your miles and your score.

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:36.180
There's no clarity of information what that actually means.

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:39.160
Right. And I think it's like, but it's a nice touch.

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:41.780
It's just that also then, where's my speedometer then?

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:45.480
And then, like, you know, like, make it a whole car thing that you're going to...

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:47.740
Yeah, give me gas. Well, they kind of do, though, right?

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:49.040
With the oil, that's what you're shooting.

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:50.480
Yeah, but that's, like, ammunition.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:52.020
Yeah, which is very strange, right?

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:54.720
We're shooting, like, oil canisters at top cars?

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:56.980
While we're painting, like, isn't that going to mix?

00:16:57.120 --> 00:16:58.480
I don't know. Oh, yeah. That's a good point.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:02.600
It's worth noting, though, two really cool things about this game.

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:08.700
So, even though we don't like that the levels change very lazily between the different

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:14.740
places in the world you go to, the backdrops are pretty nice and detailed in terms of,

00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:16.040
like... They had good backgrounds.

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:19.880
Yeah, they had no... Well, we were a fan of Karate Jam's backgrounds, too, and that game sucked.

00:17:21.620 --> 00:17:27.740
But also, the cop cars are different in each place you go to.

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:32.380
So, like, when you're in New York, they have, like, the NYPD cop cars, and then they have,

00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:34.400
like, the bus-like looking...

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:36.320
They even say, courtesy, respect, professionalism.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:38.360
Really? No, I don't...

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:42.600
I just... You said it so matter-of-factly that I was inclined to believe you.

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:44.440
They even changed the colors and things like that.

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:45.580
And actually, yeah, you know what?

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:48.420
I'm looking at the gameplay now, and that's not a cat.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:50.160
It's a cat. It's, like...

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:51.200
It's kind of like...

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:52.580
It's a cat with, like, a goat...

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:53.520
Like, a Finnish sign.

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:55.440
Like, yeah. It doesn't look like a cat to me, though.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:57.840
It almost looks like a baby with a checkered flag or something.

00:17:58.060 --> 00:18:00.680
And I guess either way, you still wouldn't want to hit it, you know?

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:01.960
I guess. But it's a cat.

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:03.240
You wouldn't want to run that over.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:06.520
But... One thing I also wanted to mention...

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:07.420
And I made a note about this.

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:13.020
The music in this game is a little different than the arcade version.

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:19.920
But it features a remix of the song Highway Star by Deep Purple.

00:18:20.540 --> 00:18:22.340
I did not notice that.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:23.540
Right. I mean, you know, I can't even think of the music.

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:25.900
Like, you know, not like an 8-bit remix, essentially.

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:28.520
You know, like, nothing crazy or anything.

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:32.280
And it definitely wasn't, like, signed off by Deep Purple to be like, yeah, yeah, use

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:33.600
our song in this obscure game.

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:38.140
But I just thought, like, that's one of the earliest examples I can think of of, like,

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:39.260
a license... Copyright infringement?

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:42.340
Yeah, yeah, exactly. Like, a popular song.

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:44.820
Yeah. Because that song would have been popular around that time.

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:46.320
True. I guess.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:49.420
Being used in a video game, you know?

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:51.300
Huh. Yeah, I can't think of anything.

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:53.580
Yeah, Mario didn't really steal from anybody.

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:59.160
There's another track called, like, Flow Waltzer.

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:00.600
Flow Waltzer, yeah.

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:01.720
Yeah, and you know about this one?

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:04.080
Radiohead, right? Maybe.

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:05.900
I don't know. No.

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:06.960
No? Okay, cool.

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:09.740
I thought that you were just, like, dead serious.

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:12.860
I'm sorry. I don't know. Like, I go to, like, the tape sessions or something like that.

00:19:12.860 --> 00:19:17.540
You know, you're pulling, like, a deep cut on me because apparently it's a, um...

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:22.140
That's what the flag-waving cat is, like, known as, is a Flow Waltzer.

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:24.860
And that's what happens when you hit one of them.

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:26.860
And so it plays that song.

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:28.480
That's why they're, like... This is all, like...

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:30.340
This is all there's, like, lore that we never found out.

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:31.780
It's not in the manual. It's not...

00:19:31.780 --> 00:19:32.640
It's, like, made-up lore?

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:36.880
Or is there, like, a cultural phenomenon of cats getting in the way of cars?

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:42.580
No, the cats in this game are named a Flow Waltzer because the name of the song

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:45.240
that plays when you hit them is the Flow Waltzer song.

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:51.520
Okay? Which is, like, literally translates to I stepped on the cat.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:52.820
I see.

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:57.440
And that's, like, apparently, like, a very popular, like, Japanese song.

00:19:57.620 --> 00:20:02.080
Of course it is. Like, not popular, like, pop music, but, like, Japanese folklore.

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:04.580
Yeah. Sure. So it has that going for it.

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:08.540
Got it. And we talked about this a little bit.

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:12.020
We mentioned the Honda City sponsorship and everything like that.

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:20.400
I happened to find the 1985 Honda City hatchback that the game is referencing for sale.

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:22.220
Comes with a little mini scooter.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:25.000
And... You don't get to use the scooter in the game.

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:27.320
Yeah. You don't get to use the scooter in the game.

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:29.000
This is only for the real-life car.

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:33.640
It's worth mentioning that the car sold at auction in 2018 for $14,000.

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:36.160
So... That's pretty affordable.

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:40.560
Yeah. It feels pretty affordable for a car from 1985 that looked pretty brand new.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:43.500
I mean, I'm sure it's not, like, that new.

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:47.060
Yeah. No, no. I'm just saying, like, it wasn't like, you know, oh, well, I forgot to tell you that,

00:20:47.140 --> 00:20:48.320
like, one of the seats is off.

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:50.460
You know? Like, everything's there in condition.

00:20:50.740 --> 00:20:52.480
I actually like this car.

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:54.500
I think it was neat that they...

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:55.680
It's very aesthetic.

00:20:56.080 --> 00:20:57.700
Yeah, yeah. But, you know, like...

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:00.840
I just think it's interesting that they actually got, like, in.

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:05.220
They were like, okay, was the game first or was the Honda City hatchback first?

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:06.480
Yeah. In terms of, like...

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:08.980
They made the car based off of the car in this game?

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:11.680
Or, like, which part of it was designed first?

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:15.340
Did they make the game and then say, like, oh, you know, like, we should see if Honda

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:16.880
wants in on this.

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:20.440
Right, I hear you. And, like, we'll put their own thing in the game and that'll help them, like, sell

00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:21.740
a bunch of Honda City hatchbacks.

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:26.700
Or was the Honda City hatchback so popular that they just felt like they should make this

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:27.780
game and include it?

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:29.640
I feel like it's gotta be the second one.

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:31.920
Because I don't know how much, like...

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:34.320
Because this was... Back then, video games were really just played by kids.

00:21:34.520 --> 00:21:35.440
Yeah, I don't... So...

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:36.480
That makes it...

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:39.940
That makes me think that neither could be true.

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:40.960
Yeah, maybe not too, yeah.

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:43.300
Because there is zero crossover in these audiences.

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:46.540
But I feel like if it was in the arcades, that's a different audience.

00:21:46.540 --> 00:21:49.180
Yeah, that's a different one. Than, like, the NES and Famicom.

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:50.980
And it could also just be for no reason.

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:52.280
Maybe just someone who...

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:54.480
A developer liked that car and just, like, yeah, I'll put it in there.

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:56.060
Without a motive.

00:21:56.700 --> 00:21:58.580
Yeah, but you still have to pay for the licensing.

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:00.500
Yeah, I don't know.

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:04.680
Yeah, that's true. I mean, this game was popular enough in Japan that it also got a commercial.

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:11.020
Oh. Which I'm not gonna play here like we usually do, but if you go into the show notes, it'll

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:16.540
definitely be there. So check that out if you just, like, really want to get your City Connection cinematic

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:21.520
universe, like, locked down and understand the difference between Clarice and the blonde

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:22.360
guy. Brett.

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:25.580
All right. Any straight thoughts?

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:27.100
I do. Yeah, please.

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:29.880
One thing that, you know, the backgrounds are good.

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:34.280
The foregrounds convey a certain amount of information.

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:37.480
But the fact that...

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:44.780
I don't know if you guys had this, but as you're scrolling, as you are going forward and

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:48.740
the camera pans with you, it's not smooth.

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:57.900
It's populating the scene line by line in a way very, very much unnerving to my belly.

00:22:58.600 --> 00:22:59.460
Like, it's...

00:22:59.460 --> 00:23:00.760
Do you guys have this?

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:01.880
I think... Yes, definitely.

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:05.680
I didn't really think about it too much until you mentioned it, but absolutely it was there.

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:10.720
But I thought you were going to say that also it feels like, and it could be an optical

00:23:10.720 --> 00:23:15.140
illusion, but it feels like the enemy cars are sort of scrolling with it.

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:17.480
Like, they're going faster if I'm driving in the same direction as them.

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:18.940
If I turn around, they go slower because they're scrolling backwards.

00:23:18.940 --> 00:23:20.940
That was another thing that kind of screwed with me.

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:29.440
Yeah. There's a perspective issue there, but it almost, for some reason, it goes away, like everything, if you're just focusing on your car.

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:31.340
But you should never do that.

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:33.260
You know what I mean?

00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:39.160
Like, you need to know what's going on with the rest of the screen, but I agree mostly

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:42.660
with the sentiment that Joe shared at the end there, that like, when you're going away

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:46.040
from the other cop cars, they almost like freeze in time.

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:47.340
Yeah. It's very strange.

00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:50.380
Because like, they're going forward, but you're sort of dragging them backwards because

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:52.520
they're like connected to the screen scroll somehow.

00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:55.860
And it's very clear that you're going faster than the cop cars.

00:23:56.020 --> 00:23:56.840
Yes. At all times.

00:23:57.100 --> 00:24:00.540
Like, you're the speed demon and they're like trying to catch up.

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:07.780
The only instance in which like there is an objective like point that the cop cars are

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:10.120
is when they're like falling from one tier to the other.

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:15.580
There they, you know, they obey the laws of gravity, but any other circumstance, yeah.

00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:18.400
At like point two speed.

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:22.580
Yeah, exactly. Did you ever feel like you were playing Grand Theft Auto while you were playing this game?

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:24.820
No. Being chased by the cops and...

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:28.160
I didn't feel like they were chasing, but they're just sort of there.

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:31.540
Yeah. They aren't really chasing, are they?

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:33.060
Yeah. It's a lot of wrong with this.

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:35.800
But if you hit triangle, you do get out of the car, which I know a lot of people probably

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:37.880
didn't notice because there was a triangle button on the controller.

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:38.980
Now, okay, that's a good question.

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:41.900
If you get out of the car, do you get Claris or do you get the blonde guy?

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:44.000
That's the only way to know if you're playing the arcade.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:45.780
You get the scooter. Yeah.

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:46.980
You get out on the scooter.

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:52.140
And it shouldn't be understated that like turning sucks so much because you're going to have

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:54.260
to do it a lot because you're missing these things.

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:59.720
And it comes down to the point where like you might be missing just the edge of a platform.

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:00.920
I'm going to be there a while.

00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:04.920
Yeah, you have to like try to just perfectly time your turnaround.

00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:06.440
And then you fall off. I almost touched it.

00:25:06.500 --> 00:25:07.360
Oh, almost touched it.

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:08.400
Like, but you don't.

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:12.660
And it really is a pain to get back onto a higher platform because the gap that you can

00:25:12.660 --> 00:25:15.220
jump through, it's hard to time it properly.

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:16.860
Yeah. And then get the right.

00:25:17.040 --> 00:25:20.700
I mean, you just have to be holding up when you jump to get the right height to get to

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:24.340
the next platform. But a lot of times, like somehow it misses and I'm just doing like a regular.

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:25.880
You hit your head on the bottom.

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:28.320
Yeah. Did anybody talk about the balloons?

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:29.820
Oh, right. Oh, I didn't.

00:25:29.880 --> 00:25:32.400
That's a big part. Yeah. The balloons are whatever, though.

00:25:32.560 --> 00:25:36.140
I mean, like. They let you skip parts of the game.

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:38.660
They let you like, yeah. It's like a warp zone kind of thing.

00:25:38.900 --> 00:25:40.580
But like without any cleverness to it.

00:25:40.700 --> 00:25:42.720
It's just get three of them and you go to the next stage.

00:25:42.740 --> 00:25:46.320
I actually at first mistook them for like what you're supposed to do.

00:25:46.540 --> 00:25:49.460
Before I read the manual, I was like, oh, I get three balloons and I go to the next stage.

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:51.140
And I thought I was like going to the next stage.

00:25:51.300 --> 00:25:53.100
And then I realized it was like a lot of order and everything.

00:25:54.860 --> 00:25:57.920
And also like if you're on a level for too long.

00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:00.200
Oh, the spikes. The spikes start showing up.

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:04.260
And that's like, is that though like the world caving in on itself or something?

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:07.460
I didn't even realize that that was because I was on a level for too long.

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:09.320
I thought it was just like, why is there suddenly a spike now?

00:26:09.400 --> 00:26:11.160
There's not been a spike for the first 20 minutes.

00:26:11.160 --> 00:26:15.320
Well, I mean, it's not like too long either before they start showing up, but it's an arcade game.

00:26:15.320 --> 00:26:17.580
So the idea is to kind of like get through this fast.

00:26:17.660 --> 00:26:20.840
And that's why I wish that the whole gameplay loop was just faster in general.

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:25.440
Because it's like, if it's going to punish me for being on a stage for too long, help me.

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:27.420
Yeah. Get through this stage, you know?

00:26:27.580 --> 00:26:28.620
Like don't.

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:29.940
Do it. Yeah.

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:33.540
Don't just randomly have spikes jut out of the ground and stuff.

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:39.600
If anything, like, you know, I could see this same game being like, oh, there's going to be more cats now the longer you stay on the screen.

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:45.760
You know, like that's the kind of stuff that they punish us with rather than thinking about ways to make it like more fun.

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:49.020
They just add more elements to the screen.

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:50.820
Maximalist. All right.

00:26:50.960 --> 00:26:56.600
If nobody has anything else, I'm going to very quickly talk about some sequels and spinoffs.

00:26:56.740 --> 00:27:00.140
Of course. Because there has to be in the City Connection Cinematic Universe.

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:03.100
Released in December of 1985.

00:27:03.760 --> 00:27:08.160
Okay. That's three years before this game came out on the NES.

00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:15.100
City Connection did come to the United States in the arcades, but it was not called City Connection.

00:27:15.420 --> 00:27:18.220
It was called Cruisin'.

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:19.440
Just Cruisin'.

00:27:19.820 --> 00:27:29.320
Cruisin'. Okay. Apparently pretty rare, but no connection to the Cruisin' in the USA or Cruisin' around the world series.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:30.560
Cruisin' for a bruisin'.

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:35.140
Yeah. No connection to any other words that involve Cruisin'.

00:27:35.140 --> 00:27:36.120
No connection to the city.

00:27:36.260 --> 00:27:37.800
Because it's also spelled differently, though.

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:47.620
Cruisin' in the USA and stuff like that puts the apostrophe where the I is, whereas this one put the apostrophe at the N, after the N.

00:27:47.620 --> 00:27:50.020
I've only seen it at the N.

00:27:50.300 --> 00:27:52.360
Nah. Wait.

00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:55.820
Say that again? Like, so Cruisin', right?

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:57.020
This game.

00:27:57.120 --> 00:27:58.780
Yeah. Has the apostrophe at the N.

00:27:59.540 --> 00:28:01.120
You know, so there's no G.

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:02.680
Right. As opposed to...

00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:06.040
Cruisin' in the USA does it like...

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:09.620
Um... Like, Cruisin' and then apostrophe NG?

00:28:09.820 --> 00:28:12.120
Yeah. No. Cruisin' and then apostrophe N.

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:13.300
And then that's N. Oh.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:14.780
That's just... That's not correct.

00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:16.800
That's weird. But it's kind of cool, right?

00:28:16.900 --> 00:28:20.120
Like, Cruisin'. I guess that is correct, but then there should be an apostrophe on both sides of the N, right?

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:21.460
Because you're getting rid of the I and the G.

00:28:21.460 --> 00:28:22.840
Well, sure. Yeah. You're getting rid of the G.

00:28:23.180 --> 00:28:24.440
They don't care, essentially.

00:28:24.560 --> 00:28:25.700
Put quotes around the N.

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:32.920
Yeah. This is a long conversation about Cruisin', but I just was thinking that that was interesting

00:28:32.920 --> 00:28:37.260
that there was a game called Cruisin' before Cruisin' series, you know?

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:39.900
Fun. And then...

00:28:40.300 --> 00:28:49.780
Oh. So, developed by Studio Runba and released for Japanese mobile phones in 2004, City Connection

00:28:49.780 --> 00:28:51.140
Rocket came out.

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:52.280
Okay? Yeah.

00:28:52.380 --> 00:28:54.960
It was available from Jaleco through the iMode.

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:58.680
It must have been some cell phone or something in 2004.

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:00.300
Only Japan, of course.

00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:07.480
The game places Clarice as a spy for a secret organization to capture criminal leaders from

00:29:07.480 --> 00:29:14.080
around the world. Rather than painting sections of the road, Clarice must now collect briefcases placed

00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:17.580
in each stage while avoiding police cars and other types of enemies.

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:19.860
Seems like a step backwards.

00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:24.260
Yeah, that's... Now you're just collecting objects on the screen instead of, like, touching the entire screen.

00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:26.400
Yeah. I prefer the touching things.

00:29:26.660 --> 00:29:30.080
Yeah. At least there's, like, some kind of, like, inherent difficulty of, like, make sure you

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:30.880
go to everything.

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:32.240
And more importantly, Splatoon.

00:29:32.380 --> 00:29:34.460
This one's, like, don't do anything.

00:29:34.660 --> 00:29:36.360
Yeah. Just get the briefcases.

00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:37.940
Please do not go anywhere else.

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:41.840
There's police. There's bad people all over the roads.

00:29:42.500 --> 00:29:48.560
All right. Well, there might be bad people all over the roads, but let's find out if there will be

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:51.180
essential games added to this list.

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:54.940
It's time for the essential games list.

00:29:57.740 --> 00:29:58.640
Not essential.

00:29:59.060 --> 00:30:01.920
All right. I guess I can give a little more of that.

